title op3: the rival that only appears after the parent is on the docket

# Proposition 16.4's hazard, built to fire.  This is the fixture that
# tells a one-phase implementation from a two-phase one, and it is
# the only one of the ten where a plausible wrong implementation
# gives a plausible wrong ANSWER instead of an error.
#
# Off-of rebuilds /top as /peak.  Inside it there are two
# directories where base had one:
#
#   alpha holds f and g with their original dnodes -- red.
#   beta  holds f and g at the same leaves, with fresh dnodes and
#         DIFFERENT content.  Fresh dnodes mean no red; different
#         bytes mean not even a yellow vote.  The only thing tying
#         inner to beta is that the leaves match.
#
# The trick rests on an asymmetry in Definition 16.3 worth saying out
# loud: a name match can VOTE but it cannot NOMINATE.  Candidates
# come from two generators only -- a child's RED edge nominating its
# landing place's parent, and a deletion and an addition that are
# siblings under a parent already in the relation.  (Yellow was a
# third generator in the first draft and was removed by the kernel
# lane: a nomination is the inverted, whole-tree question the lazy
# pairwise yellow oracle cannot answer.  The removal makes this
# fixture sharper, not weaker -- beta now has exactly one route in.)
#
# So beta reaches the docket only through the sibling generator,
# under the pair (base /top, off-of /peak), and a naive
# implementation reaches that route one round too late.
#
# NAIVE ITERATION:
#   round 1  f's and g's red anchors nominate (inner, alpha).  It is
#            the only candidate, its voters are unanimous, so inner
#            is believed to have moved to alpha.  inner can now vote
#            as a child, so top is believed to have moved to peak.
#   round 2  (top, peak) is now corresponded, so the sibling
#            generator finally offers (inner, beta) -- and inner's
#            voters are accounted for there too, by name match.  The
#            round-1 belief was made against a docket that was
#            missing a rival.  Correspondence only ever grows, so the
#            iteration LOOKS monotone.  The guard it violates is
#            uniqueness, which is evaluated against the candidate set
#            and is not monotone in it.
#
# TWO PHASES:
#   phase 1  closes candidates and judges nothing.  (top, peak) is a
#            sibling pair under the green root, so (inner, alpha) and
#            (inner, beta) are both in the closure before any belief
#            is formed.
#   phase 2  judges bottom-up.  At height 1, inner has two equally
#            good targets, so the unique-target guard blocks and
#            inner is not believed.  At height 2, top's only child is
#            the directory inner, whose vote requires a believed move
#            of its own; it has none, so it abstains, and top has no
#            voters and concludes nothing.
#
# Hand-worked: clean, and NOTHING is believed.  The gold is the plain
# decay -- /top and /inner die, /peak, /alpha and /beta are born --
# with the two files that kept their dnodes relinked under alpha.  A
# one-phase engine reuses dnode 2 for /peak and dnode 3 for /alpha
# instead, which is a strictly different gold section, so the fixture
# cannot pass both ways.
#
# ONE WORDING QUESTION FOR THE THEORY LANE, raised because this
# fixture is where it bites.  Definition 16.3 says the sibling
# generator works "under an already-CORRESPONDED parent," while
# Proposition 16.4 says "scoped sibling pairs under pairs already
# PRESENT."  Only the second reading makes the proposition's own
# argument work: on the first reading (top, peak) would have to be
# BELIEVED before (inner, beta) could be nominated, belief would have
# to precede closure, and phase 1 would be judging -- which is the
# hazard the proposition exists to remove.  The second reading is
# also the only one under which phase 1 is a fixed point at all;
# red, green and red-anchor pairs are computable in a single pass,
# so the sibling generator is the sole reason there is anything to
# iterate.  This fixture assumes Proposition 16.4's wording.  If
# Definition 16.3 means what it says, this fixture's gold is wrong
# AND the proposition is unsound, so it is worth one sentence to fix.
#
# Last, the uncomfortable part of the right answer, recorded because
# it will be argued: two phases here refuse a move that a person
# reading the trees would probably call obvious.  That is the price
# of judging once against everything, and it is the honest price --
# the rival is real, and the round-1 belief was luck.

tree base
  1-1: /
    2-100: top/
      3-100: inner/
        4-100: f = fv0
        5-100: g = gv0

tree onto
  1-1: /
    2-100: top/
      3-100: inner/
        4-100: f = fv0
        5-100: g = gv0

tree offof
  1-1: /
    20-300: peak/
      30-300: alpha/
        4-100: f = fv0
        5-100: g = gv0
      31-300: beta/
        41-300: f = fv9
        42-300: g = gv9

#
# WORKED OUTCOME (by hand, before any implementation ran)
#   believed moves:  NONE.  /top/inner has two candidates -- alpha by
#                    red anchor, beta by the sibling generator -- and
#                    its voters are accounted for in both, red into
#                    alpha and name match into beta, so the
#                    unique-target guard blocks.  /top then has no
#                    voter at all, its only child being a directory
#                    with no believed move of its own
#   deltas:          none
#   decisions:       /top and /top/inner die; /peak, /alpha and /beta
#                    are born fresh; f and g keep their dnodes and
#                    are relinked under /peak/alpha.  A ONE-PHASE
#                    engine instead reuses dnode 2 for /peak and
#                    dnode 3 for /alpha
#   conflicts:       none, onto is quiet
#   emission:        link the three new directories parent-first,
#                    rename f and g into alpha, link beta's two
#                    files, then unlink inner and top children-first
# --- expected result, worked by hand 2026-08-31 ---
expect clean

tree expected
  1-1: /
    20-300: peak
      30-300: alpha
        4-100: f = fv0
        5-100: g = gv0
      31-300: beta
        41-300: f = fv9
        42-300: g = gv9
